          %h2#proofs_via_adjoints Proofs via adjoints
          %p
            The properties 1., 2. of Proposition
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PreservationOfUnionsAndIntersectionsOfSets"}
            may be proved by appeal to fundamental relationships between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+image"} direct image
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
            and the like, which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theorists
            call
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            (similar in form to adjoints in linear algebra). The advantage of this type of proof is that, despite its utter simplicity, it generalizes to much wider contexts (beyond elementary classical set theory).
          %h3#direct_images_preserve_unions_inverse_images_preserve_intersections Direct images preserve unions, inverse images preserve intersections
          %p The first such relationship is:
          %p
