                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
                  is also called the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
              %li
                %p
                  the middle morphism is also called
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+extension"} context extension
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\ast
                  is also called the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
            %p
              All this restricts to the (-1)-truncated objects by composing the left adjoint with
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-truncation"} (-1)-truncation
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperdoctrine"} hyperdoctrine
              and (under “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"}> propositions as types
              ”) may think of
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  the left adjoint
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
