            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits+and+colimits+by+example"} limits and colimits by example
          %h2#references References
          %p Textbook accounts include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Apostol"}> Tom Apostol
                ,
                %a{:href => "http://www.ru.ac.bd/wp-content/uploads/sites/25/2019/03/205_04_Apostol-Mathematical-Analysis-1973.pdf#page=57"} p. 44
                in:
                %em Mathematical Analysis
                1973 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.ru.ac.bd/wp-content/uploads/sites/25/2019/03/205_04_Apostol-Mathematical-Analysis-1973.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Lee00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+M.+Lee"}> John M. Lee
                , Exercise A.4 in:
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Introduction to topological manifolds
              %p
                Second edition: Springer, 2011. ISBN: 978-1-4419-7939-1 (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-7940-7"}> doi:10.1007/978-1-4419-7940-7
                , errata
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://sites.math.washington.edu/~lee/Books/ITM/errata.pdf"} pdf
          %p The slogan of Lawvere on logic and adjoint functors appears in
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere05
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+William+Lawvere"}> F. William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em An elementary theory of the category of sets
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/11/tr11abs.html"} link
          %p based on
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere69
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adjointness+in+Foundations"} Adjointness in Foundations
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/reprints/articles/16/tr16abs.html"} TAC
          %p
            The story of Lebesgue’s slip that projections commute with intersections, in attempting to prove that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projections"} projections
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+sets"} Borel sets
            are Borel, is well explained here:
          %ul
            %li
              Dave L. Renfro,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Mikhail Y. Suslin and Lebesgue’s error
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://mathforum.org/kb/message.jspa?messageID=4966721"} here
          %p
            The analogy between adjoint functors and adjoints in linear algebra becomes very strong when jacking up from Hilbert spaces to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-Hilbert+spaces"} 2-Hilbert spaces
          %ul
            %li#JB97
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+C.+Baez"}> John C. Baez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Higher-Dimensional Algebra II. 2-Hilbert Spaces
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1006/aima.1997.1617"} https://doi.org/10.1006/aima.1997.1617
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001870897916170"}> Elsevier link
              )
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  There is a famous story of how
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Yakovlevich+Suslin"} Suslin
                  discovered an error in an argument of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue"} Lebesgue
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Yakovlevich+Suslin"} Suslin
                  was led to develop some of the basics of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descriptive+set+theory"} descriptive set theory
                  %a{:href => "http://mathforum.org/kb/message.jspa?messageID=4966721"} here
                  and
                  %a{:href => "http://mathforum.org/kb/message.jspa?messageID=4967733"} here
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
